---
import '../styles/global.css';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navigation from '../components/Navigation.astro';
import BackButton from '../components/BackButton.astro';
import CognitiveShuffle from '../components/CognitiveShuffle.tsx';
---

<BaseLayout title="Cognitive Shuffle" description="Quiet your racing mind by visualizing random words. A gentle cognitive exercise to help you fall asleep.">
  <div class="page">
    <div class="page__header">
      <BackButton />
      <h1 class="page__title">Cognitive Shuffle</h1>
      <p class="page__desc">Let your mind wander through random images</p>
    </div>
    <CognitiveShuffle client:load />
  </div>

  <article class="article-guide">
    <div class="article-guide__inner">
      <h2>The Science of Cognitive Shuffling</h2>
      <p>
        If you have ever found yourself lying awake in bed, exhausted yet unable to drift off, your prefrontal cortex is likely stuck in an analytical loop. When you review your day, worry about tomorrow's obligations, or stress about not sleeping, your brain remains in an active problem-solving state. Sleep onset cannot naturally occur while the mind perceives unresolved challenges or threats in its environment.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Cognitive Shuffle, clinically termed <strong>Serial Diverse Imagining (SDI)</strong>, is a scientifically backed cognitive technique designed to disrupt this cycle. Pioneered by cognitive scientist Dr. Luc Beaudoin at Simon Fraser University, the method guides the brain into a state of benign, unstructured mentation that closely mimics natural sleep onset.
      </p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <strong>Core Principle:</strong> Analytical thinking signals wakefulness to the brain. Disjointed, random mental imagery signals to the thalamus that it is safe to initiate the sleep state.
      </div>

      <h3>How Cognitive Shuffling Works in the Brain</h3>
      <p>
        As humans transition from wakefulness into Stage 1 light sleep, brainwave patterns shift from high-frequency beta waves down into alpha and theta rhythms. During this natural transition (called the hypnagogic state), our thoughts lose their logical structure and begin to drift through fragmented, nonsensical scenes or micro-dreams.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you consciously visualize random, emotionally neutral objects in rapid succession (such as an apple, an anchor, an apron, an arrow), two critical neurological shifts occur:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cognitive Displacement:</strong> Your working memory has limited capacity. By actively visualizing vivid sensory details of harmless objects, you leave no cognitive bandwidth for anxious ruminations or planning.</li>
        <li><strong>Somnolent Signaling:</strong> Because coherent, linear thoughts keep the brain alert, feeding the cortex fragmented and unrelated concepts mimics the natural onset of sleep. The brain recognizes this randomness as a signal that the day is over and lowers cortical arousal.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="guide-card-grid">
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>1. Pick a Word or Category</h4>
          <p>Choose a calming seed word or let our interactive tool provide random, imagery-rich words automatically.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>2. Visualize Without Judging</h4>
          <p>Picture each item in your mind for 5 to 10 seconds. Notice its color, shape, texture, or motion, then move to the next.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>3. Allow Thoughts to Drift</h4>
          <p>Do not force a storyline. The more unrelated and random the images are, the faster your analytical brain powers down.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Step-by-Step Guide: Practicing in Bed</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Get comfortable:</strong> Lie in your preferred sleeping posture. Dim all bedroom lights and turn down phone brightness using our dark-mode interface.</li>
        <li><strong>Observe the prompt:</strong> Look at the word presented on screen, or close your eyes and listen to your internal visualization.</li>
        <li><strong>Construct a mental image:</strong> Spend several seconds visualizing the item with rich detail. For example, if the word is "Lighthouse," visualize waves crashing against dark granite stones under a warm revolving light.</li>
        <li><strong>Let it fade and advance:</strong> Do not cling to the image. Let it dissolve and welcome the next word. If your mind wanders back to daily worries, gently acknowledge the thought without self-judgment and return your focus to the next visual cue.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="guide-disclaimer">
        <strong>Wellness Notice:</strong> Cognitive Shuffling is a behavioral sleep-hygiene exercise created for general wellness and bedtime relaxation. It is not intended to diagnose, treat, or cure chronic clinical insomnia, sleep apnea, or other medical sleep disorders. If sleep difficulties persist for several weeks, please consult a qualified healthcare professional.
      </div>

      <h3>Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is Cognitive Shuffling?</summary>
          <p>Cognitive Shuffling is a mental sleep technique that involves visualizing a sequence of random, emotionally neutral objects. It replaces racing thoughts with fragmented imagery, mimicking the natural mental patterns that occur just before falling asleep.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Who invented Cognitive Shuffling?</summary>
          <p>The technique was developed by Dr. Luc P. Beaudoin, an adjunct professor of cognitive science and education at Simon Fraser University in Canada, based on his research into sleep onset cognition and serial diverse imagining.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How long does it take for Cognitive Shuffling to work?</summary>
          <p>Most individuals report feeling noticeable drowsiness within 5 to 15 minutes of continuous practice. For some, sleep occurs so smoothly that they do not remember finishing the exercise.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why does this work better than counting sheep?</summary>
          <p>Counting sheep is repetitive and rhythmic, which can actually allow anxious thoughts to run in the background. Cognitive Shuffling requires just enough active imagination to occupy the working memory while remaining free of stress or emotional weight.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What should I do if my mind wanders during the exercise?</summary>
          <p>Mind wandering is completely natural, especially when you are tired or stressed. When you catch yourself thinking about work or chores, do not get frustrated. Simply press next on the tool or introduce a new random object into your imagination.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <Navigation slot="nav" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    max-width: 32rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1rem 1rem 6rem;
  }

  .page__header {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .page__title {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0.25rem;
    color: var(--color-drift-text);
  }

  .page__desc {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-drift-text-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
